# mulligan-tucks-a-card already proves one London mulligan; this is two in a
# row, proving londonTuckCount's own escalation (mulligan.go): the first
# mulligan costs one card, the second costs two, not one-plus-one. Each
# mulligan reshuffles the ENTIRE current hand back into the library before
# redrawing seven fresh (mulligan, mulligan.go), so the card tucked by the
# first mulligan is back in the pool the second one draws from -- only the
# second mulligan's own two tucked cards end up on the bottom for good; a
# CardID is stable across all of it (ADR-0009), so `queue tuck` can keep
# naming the original fixture ids throughout.
queue keephand false
# human: first mulligan
queue tuck 1
# the card the first mulligan tucks
queue keephand true
# ai: keeps its own hand in the same round
queue keephand false
# human: second mulligan
queue tuck 2,3
# the two cards the second mulligan tucks
queue keephand true
# human: keeps the redrawn, five-card hand
mulligan human
